A||||9||johnt671||High current draw w/ fan is on||||5||
Z||000000||johnt671||06-16-2001||04:09 PM||||Car is an 84 SE w/air.  When I turned the non working a/c to activate the cooling fan the voltage gauge dropped into the discharge side.  I ordered a new fan switch but the last owner had spliced the wire to the switch into something else and I can't find the wire for sure.  So I wired a switch in so I could turn it on myself, but I still have the same problem.  I bypassed the fan relay and spliced into the red wire going into the relay to the switch and then spliced the other end of the switch to the fan motor.  It works but I still have the same problem with high current draw.  Anyone got any ideas?  I have a back up motor and bracket from a non a/c car.  Will this work ok?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Seanh||06-16-2001||04:23 PM||||Sounds like your alternator is the problem actually. My car has done this for 2 years now, and still does, and Iv never had a problem with a dead battery. When I put a voltmeter on it, it shows 14.4v while runing. Could just be a jumpy voltmeter on our cars also. Mine jumps with the bass from my system, turn signals, brake light, head lights, everything!||||reg||1
Z||000002||johnt671||06-16-2001||05:25 PM||||Just put a new alt. on this morning as I was getting a jumpy reading even w/o the fan on.  Now it reads steady excpt when I turn the fan on.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||06-16-2001||05:29 PM||||Well, the fan does pull quite a bit of power. One option is to get an aftermarket fan, that maybe pulls less current if your worried about that. I have my system setup so that it will turn on with the stock fan switch, or it will turn on when I flip a switch on my dash. I too notice it drop greatly when I turn the fan on. It seems to kinda go up again once the fan is spinning up to speed. It will pull alot of power when it is getting started and up to speed. Permacool makes a fan that is pretty much a direct drop onto the Fiero rad. Anyone have that link handy? How much do they cost?||||reg||1
Z||000004||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||06-17-2001||12:02 AM||||Did you get an alternator for an A/C Fiero?  <P>The standard alternator is good for about 65 amps.  The A/C version is about 95 amps.<P>I got the wrong one once and it dropped at idle whenever the head lights, A/C, and sterero were all on together.  It died pretty quickly too, less than 1 year before it started to interfere with the idle of the car.<P>Here's how to tell if you got the right alternator . . . Did you pay $60 or $100?<P>A/C alternators are about $110 for a good brand rebuilt (I only buy Autolite, but that's just me).  <P>The autolite A/C capable alternator in my car does not change in voltage unless you turn off the key or the idle drops below 500 rpm.  I have never seen less than 13 volts with the engine running.  It just sits at 14.5 all the time.<P>Hopefully, you bought the right alternator . . .<P>||||reg||3
Z||000005||johnt671||06-17-2001||08:01 AM||||I made sure it was for a/c.  When I asked for it he asked if it had a/c and they have the computer screen set up so you can read it with them.  I also got the more expensive one.  I guess that dropping to 13 with the fan on is normal.  I was worried about frying the new alt. as it is a PITA to install. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierohoho||06-17-2001||09:42 AM||||Johnt671, I remember a conversation with elfiero about the same problem. <P>I think he said the way the power distribution is wired it puts the alt gauge on the wrong side of the battery and they corrected this on later models. <P>By wrong side I think its too close to where the most power is pulled from and is reading off the alt not the battery. <P>My 84 does the same thing and thats after I rebuilt the alt. <P>Like Seanh said you still get good volts at the battery so I would leave well enough alone until something goes bad.<P>Any comment elfiero?<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000007||William Federle||06-17-2001||03:49 PM||||I had a similar problem last summer. It turned out that the magnets of the fan motor had bound up the fan armature. A fusible link burned in two down by the starter motor when the fan siezed up. I had to get the car towed home. I got a new flexlite fan and adapted it to the fan mounting frame and I moved both fusible links from the starter to an easier to get to location. <P>I hope you don't get stuck somewhere.<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 06-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||06-17-2001||04:00 PM||||I wouldn't worry. I can drive all thru town for a couple hours without the fan on, and the car stays right at 175. It drops to 165 on the highway without the fan. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||johnt671||06-17-2001||06:17 PM||||THanks.  I don't use the fan often either, only once in a while if I get stuck in traffic. I have a 180 degree stat in so it stays preety cool most of the time.  I may see if I can mount the fan and bracet I have from a non a/c car in, or an after market fan but I sound safe for a while.  I,ve been stuck in N&gt;J&gt; twice with the car and don't want a repeat.||||reg||1
